{"duration_api_ms":183548,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"88620b29-d4ad-416d-b703-7802f21b1141","total_cost_usd":0.6695973999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":18,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31562,"cache_read_input_tokens":320047,"output_tokens":17651,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":10167},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31562,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1048,"cache_read_input_tokens":46165,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2255,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2255},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":18,"outputTokens":17651,"cacheReadInputTokens":320047,"cacheCreationInputTokens":31562,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.6695973999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":10167,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":9,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I fixed the caching: a `save_fact` now adds only the new fact to the cache instead of re-caching the whole prompt. I checked this with a local simulation of the cache, not against the real API. There was no key, and external calls were off limits.\n\n**Cause.** `build_system()` built the whole system prompt as one block with one cache marker. The cache works on prefixes, so each new fact changed that block and the whole prompt (~1050 tokens) was written again at the write price. That matches the log: after every `save_fact`, `cache_read_input_tokens: 0` and `cache_creation_input_tokens` ≈ 1040–1100.\n\n**The colleague's fix alone wouldn't help.** Splitting into \"instructions + the rest\" doesn't work here. Sonnet 4.5 doesn't cache a prefix shorter than 1024 tokens. By the log, tools + instructions are about 920–950 tokens (user 77's first message cost exactly 1024 for the whole prompt). So a marker after the instructions never writes anything.\n\n**What I did** (`bot.py`, `build_system`):\n- The system prompt is now a list of blocks: the static instructions with their own marker, then the athlete context (date, profile, zones, goal, language), then one block per fact. The marker goes on the last fact.\n- Facts are only ever appended to the end, so the new request differs from the previous one by one block. The API looks back over earlier block boundaries, finds the previous cache entry and reads it. Only the new fact is written, ~11–12 tokens in the simulation.\n- **Condition:** facts must stay append-only. Editing, deleting, deduplicating or picking \"relevant\" facts breaks the cache again. This is written in the code comment. I removed the `(none yet)` placeholder because the first fact would have replaced it.\n- I left tool filtering alone, as `NOTES.md` asks.\n\n**Check.** I added `tests/test_cache_layout.py` (5 tests, all pass, no network calls). It replays \"message → `save_fact` → message\" against a simplified cache model calibrated to `usage.jsonl`. Old layout: 0 read / 1039 written after a memory write. New layout: 1026 read / 11 written. The colleague's two-segment layout fails the same way as the old one, which a separate test checks. Run them with `python3 -m unittest discover tests`. I also updated the README.\n\n**In production:** after a `save_fact`, `logs/usage.jsonl` should show a read about the size of the previous prompt and a write of about 10–30 tokens. If it shows 0 read / ~1000 written again, something in the prefix stopped being byte-stable.\n\nFrom the colleague's note, these parts helped: the prefix-hash diagnosis, a separate block for the unchanging instructions, loading all facts in a stable order instead of picking some, keeping tools stable, and checking via the `usage` fields. The two-segment layout itself didn't carry over because of the 1024-token minimum. Tool filtering I skipped since the bot already has it. Details are in `ANSWER.md`.","ttft_ms":2324,"type":"result","duration_ms":184881,"uuid":"d751fdc7-e111-4aea-96ad-9ecd0e595965","ttft_stream_ms":974,"time_to_request_ms":30,"first_content_frame_ms":975,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":18,"outputTokens":17651,"cacheReadInputTokens":320047,"cacheCreationInputTokens":31562,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.6695973999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":10167,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}}}
